help.body:
## The four tabs

- **Library** is the first page of Play. It lists the example runs and your own, with the detail of the one you choose. **Load** opens a run in Re-play. The **Tags** button (key **T**) shows a cloud of topics: the bigger a topic, the more runs are about it. Click topics to show only the runs that have all of them; **Clear filters** shows every run again.
- **Re-play** shows the run you loaded, or the one you opened last (the button with the arrow loads it).
- **Create** is where you build your own run: write a task, choose a pattern, add and connect agents, then press Run.
- **Result** shows the answer and the numbers of a run. It is light green when there is a result to see.
## Using the replay

- **Play and pause** start and stop the replay. **Speed** slows it down or speeds it up.
- **Previous step and Next step** move one message at a time. **The bar** lets you jump to any point.
- **The agent cards** show who is working, waiting or done. When the replay ends, an agent that never ran says **not called** (a supervisor chose not to use it) or **not reached** (the run stopped before it). Click a card to read that agent's steps, messages and output; to see why a supervisor did not call an agent, read the Supervisor's Messages or Output.
- A run from the library is a recording made with its own limits. The limits in your Settings apply to new runs, not to a recording; the Harness lab shows what they would do to it.
- **Click a step** to read its exact message.
- In a debate, a badge shows which round it is in.

### Replay speed {#replay-speed}

How fast the recorded run plays. A run is replayed from its record, so a slow one can be watched quickly and nothing is sent to a model. Speeds are 1x, 2x and so on. With the side layout one button cycles through them; the layout is chosen in Settings, Player controls.

{related: settings-player, replay-steps}

### Steps and tools {#replay-steps}

Everything the chosen agent did, one line for each step: the model call, a web search, a retry. Select a step to see its details. The player decides how far the run is revealed.

{related: replay-messages, replay-output}

### Messages {#replay-messages}

What passed between the agents: who sent what to whom. In a debate or with a supervisor you see each instruction and each answer in order.

{related: replay-steps, harness-handoff}

### Agent output {#replay-output}

The full answer of the chosen agent. A handoff cut or a reply that reached its token limit is marked here, and the whole answer is kept.

{related: replay-steps, harness-output, harness-handoff}

### Context {#replay-context}

What the chosen agent had in front of it on each model call: its instructions, the task, what other agents passed on and its own earlier messages. The number is the input tokens the provider counted, so it is exact. The coloured bar splits the call by characters, so it shows roughly where the tokens went. The context of an agent only grows during its work, which is why long runs cost more with every call.

{related: replay-steps, replay-messages, result-context}

### API calls {#replay-api}

The exact messages between this app and the provider for the chosen agent: every call in order, also the ones that failed or got no answer. A row opens to what was sent (the address, the headers and the body) and what came back (the status, the headers and the body). Your key is shown as (hidden), and so is anything that could identify your account. For a provider that uses another format than Anthropic, the tab explains what the app translates. The search box in the header also searches the calls and opens the rows that match. Runs recorded before this tab existed have no calls. A call that is still waiting for an answer when you press Stop is not recorded.

{related: replay-messages, replay-context}

### Settings of an agent {#replay-settings}

How the chosen agent was set up in this run: its role, the provider and model, web search, its instructions, its own limits and what it does at them, and the optional parts (notebook, condensing, approval, answer format and guardrails). It is read from the recording, so it shows what was true when the run was made, not what your Settings say now. The text the agent really received is on the Messages tab, and the exact request on API calls.

{related: replay-messages, replay-api}

### Warnings in a replay {#replay-warnings}

A warning means the run went on but something needed your attention: a busy provider and a new try, an answer that was cut, or a limit that stopped the run. It names the agent and, when the run recorded it, the time. Open the related section for what the setting does and how to change it.

{related: harness-retries, harness-steps, harness-cost}

### How it ended {#result-outcome}

Whether the run finished, failed, was interrupted, is waiting for your approval or was stopped by a limit (or by you), and which limit. A run stopped by a limit keeps everything done so far, and Retry carries on from the agents that finished.

{related: result-limits, harness-steps}

### Estimated cost {#result-cost}

What the run cost by the list prices in the catalog. An agent on a free Gemini key counts as free. The real bill at the provider can differ a little, so treat it as a close estimate.

{related: harness-cost, create-estimate}

### Context per call {#result-context}

The input tokens of every model call of the run, in order. The largest bar is the **largest context**: the most the model had to read at once. A run with several agents, a loop or a debate climbs, because each call carries more of what came before. Anything that shortens what the agents receive makes the bars lower.

{related: replay-context, result-cost}

### Tokens per agent {#result-agents}

What each agent used: its time, the tokens it read (input) and wrote (output), and its share of the time. Tokens are what you pay for, and the agents that write the most usually cost the most.

{related: harness-tokens, result-cost}

### Limits {#result-limits}

What the run and each agent used of their limits. A limit marked run is the limit of the whole run; an agent can have a lower one of its own, and the lower one wins. A limit that was reached is marked.

{related: harness-steps, harness-tokens, harness-cost}

### Untrusted web content {#replay-web-content}

Search results are text that came from the web, so they are marked as data, not instructions. The agent was told so, but a model can still be fooled by a page written to look like instructions. If an agent did something odd after a search, read the results here first.

{related: harness-web-content, harness-web-search}

### Waiting for approval {#play-approval}

A yellow box shows which agent is next and the text it will receive. **Approve and continue** runs the agent and the rest of the run, with the answers you already have. **Stop here** ends the run as stopped by you; the result and the replay of the steps so far stay.

The wait does not count toward the time limit, and a paused run is saved with your other runs. In the replay the step shows **Approved by you**. Only your own runs can be approved, not the library's.

{related: create-approval, harness-timeout}

## The Library

The Library is where you choose a run. The list is on the left, the detail of the chosen run on the right; on a phone the list comes first and the detail is the next screen.

### Browsing and loading runs {#library}

- The list has two tabs: **Your runs** (made in this browser) and **Library** (the examples). A card shows the title, how the run ended, when it was made, the providers and the pattern. Click anywhere on a card to see its detail; the tick box at the right of the card only ticks.
- The detail says what the run is about, how it works, what the student learns, what to watch for in the replay, the settings of each agent and the articles that explain it. Your own run also has the description you wrote.
- **Load** opens the run in Re-play, waiting at the start. **Use in Create** copies its task and design to Create. **Export** saves the run as a JSON file. **Compare with** starts a comparison. For your own runs there are also **Send** (your finished runs: it saves the file and opens an e-mail to the Agent Atlas team, and the run holds the whole task and every answer, so check it has no private information), **Delete** and **Edit description**.
- The search box in the header searches the list, including the texts of the details. It is the same box on Library, Re-play and Result.
- Tick two to four runs to compare them: a bar shows how many are ticked, and the first one you ticked is the base. See Comparing runs below.

### Importing and exporting several runs {#library-bundle}

- **Export ticked** saves the ticked runs in one file, and **Export all my runs** saves all your own. A file with several runs is a bundle; a file with one run, as Export writes it, is a bundle of one and is accepted too. Export leaves out your language, time zone and browser.
- The arrow button imports one or more files at once. A summary above the list shows, for every run, what happened: added, skipped because you already have it, saved as a copy because a different run had the same id, or refused with the reason.
- An import never replaces a run. The same id and the same content is skipped, so importing a file twice changes nothing. If the browser runs out of room, the runs saved before stay and the rest are reported as refused.

### Keyboard in the Library {#library-keys}

Keys work when the focus is in the list or the detail (or nowhere in particular), and not while you type in a field.

- **↑ ↓** move between the cards and the detail follows. **← →** switch between Your runs and Library.
- **Enter** or **L** loads the run. **Space** ticks the card. **C** compares the ticked runs. **E** exports the ticked runs and **Shift+E** exports all your own. **U** uses the run in Create. **N** edits the description of your run. **Delete** deletes your run, after asking.
- **I** imports, **O** loads the run you opened last, **/** goes to the search box.
- **Q** swaps the focus between the list and the detail. **B** goes back to the Library from Re-play or Result. **Esc** leaves the list or the detail.
- A key with nothing to act on says so under the tabs, for example Delete on an example run.

### Comparing runs {#compare-runs}

Comparing shows what is different between two to four of your runs and what that difference did. Tick the runs in the library, then press Compare. The first one you ticked is the base: the others are measured against it.

**What changed.** A list of the differences found in the recordings: the pattern, the model of each agent, web search, the notebook and condensing, the limits, the language, the provider and the task. What is the same in all the runs is not listed. Agents are matched by their id, so an agent that exists in only some runs is marked.

**What it changed.** Time, cost, tokens, steps, the largest context, notes, characters saved by condensing and how the run ended, with the difference from the base. The arrows show the direction only. A cheaper run is not better by itself, and a slower one is not worse: it depends on what you wanted.

**Quality.** The program cannot say which result is better, so it does not try. The results are shown side by side and you choose. Your choice and note are kept in this browser. If the tasks differ, the results answer different questions and the choice is hidden.

**Side by side.** The runs are played together with one set of controls. By time, all runs share one clock: a faster run finishes earlier and waits for the others. By step, every run is at the same step number. Each column shows what its agents have done so far and the time, cost and tokens used up to that moment. Side by side shows three runs at most; the table shows four.

**Limits.** Runs can be compared only when they are saved in this browser or in the library. A single change is easiest to read. When several things changed at once, the table says what changed but not which change caused which effect. One run of a task is a sample: a model can answer differently each time, so a small difference may be chance.

{related: replay-context, create-memory}

## Creating a run

- Choose a pattern: sequential, parallel, supervisor or debate. Each one starts from the agents it needs.
- Choose the provider with the tiles above the diagram: Anthropic, Gemini free (free models only) and Gemini paid (all models). You can pick more than one. A dot on a tile shows its key: green when it is loaded, yellow when it is saved but locked, none when there is no key. A tile without a key opens its key section in Settings.
- The **Default model** list sets the model of every agent. Select an agent to give it a model of its own, or set it back to Default. Agents may use different providers in one run; each agent uses the key of its provider.
- Add agents from the palette and connect them with the arrow on each box. Drag the boxes to arrange them.
- Select an agent to change its model, web search, name and instructions. A free agent is yours to define. Open **Limits of this agent** to give it its own limits (steps, tokens, tokens of one reply, web searches) and to choose what happens when it reaches one: stop the whole run, or pass on what it wrote and go on. An empty box means the limits of the run apply; the lower one always wins.
- Under the diagram you see an estimate and the limits for the run. A yellow warning appears when the design will probably reach a limit. A run stops as soon as it reaches a limit, and what happened so far is saved.
- **Load definition** and **Clear definition** replace the task and the diagram.

### Task {#create-task}

What you want the agents to do, in at most 1,000 characters. Every agent sees it. A clear task with a clear end gives the best result, and a short one costs less.

{related: create-pattern, create-estimate}

### Orchestration pattern {#create-pattern}

The pattern decides how the agents work together: who works when and who sees what. Sequential passes a result from one agent to the next, parallel lets several work at once and one combines the results, a supervisor hands out work, in a debate agents argue in rounds and a judge decides, in an evaluator loop a writer revises until an evaluator accepts, in a handoff agents pass control on with a note, a router sends the task to one specialist, and in voting several agents answer alone and a tally names the answer most of them agree on. It also decides which connections the diagram allows, so changing it clears the diagram.

{related: create-diagram, harness-steps}

### Diagram {#create-diagram}

Add agents from the palette and connect them with the arrow on each box; drag the boxes to arrange them. Select an agent to change its model, web search, name and instructions. At most 8 agents.

{related: create-pattern, create-limits}

### Limits for this run {#create-limits}

The most a run may use: steps, tokens and web searches. A run stops as soon as it reaches one, and what happened so far is saved. The values start from your Harness settings and can be changed for this one run. Limits you change stay while you move between tabs; opening a run with Use in Create starts again from your Settings.

{related: harness-steps, harness-tokens, harness-searches}

### Estimated cost {#create-estimate}

The most this run is likely to cost, worked out from the number of agents, your limits and the prices in the catalog. It is a ceiling, not a promise: a run usually costs less, and the real bill at the provider can differ a little. Web searches and a free Gemini key change it.

{related: harness-cost, harness-tokens}

### Check and run {#create-run}

Before you run, check the agents, the limits and the cost. The key of each provider the design uses must be loaded. A yellow warning says the design will probably reach a limit.

{related: create-estimate, create-limits}

### Tools of an agent {#create-tools}

A tool is something an agent can do besides writing text. Today there is one: web search. Give a tool only to the agents that need it, so a mistake or a poisoned web page can reach as little as possible (least privilege). A free agent that only reasons does not need the web.

If web search is turned off in Settings, Harness, Tools, an agent with the switch on will not search, and the editor tells you.

{related: harness-web-search, harness-web-content, create-limits}

### Router {#create-router}

A router reads the task and chooses one specialist. That specialist answers, and that answer is the result. The router chooses once. It never sees the answer and nothing comes back to it, so it is the cheapest pattern that still chooses: two calls, however many specialists there are. The arrows go from the router to each specialist it may choose.

**What the specialist gets.** Only the task. The router passes on a choice, not a note. In the replay you can read the reason the router gave.

**Why one choice is risky**

- The router decides on little information, often only the task and a short description of each specialist. A vague description gives a poor choice.
- There is no second chance. If the router picks the wrong specialist, you still get an answer, and nobody notices that it came from the wrong one.
- The router must choose someone. Agent Atlas has no "none of these". A real system adds a fallback specialist, a check of how sure the router is, or a second look at the answer.

**How it differs from the others**

- **Handoff:** control can move on again and again, as a chain. The router decides once.
- **Supervisor:** every result comes back to the boss, which can call several workers. The router never gets a result back.
- **Parallel:** all the agents work. In a router only one specialist works.

The decision is a JSON answer that the program reads. If it is not valid, the router is asked once more, and a second bad answer fails the run.

{related: create-handoff, create-pattern, create-limits}

### Voting {#create-voting}

Several agents answer the same task, each on its own. A Tally agent reads all the answers, finds the ones that agree and gives the answer most of them reached. The more voters agree, the more you can trust it. The arrows go from each voter to the tally. The voters never see each other's answers.

**Why it helps**

- One answer can be wrong by chance. When several independent answers match, a chance mistake is unlikely.
- When the voters differ, that is a useful signal too: the task may be unclear or hard.

**What it costs and where it fails**

- **Cost:** five voters cost five answers plus the Tally.
- **A majority is not the truth.** Voters of the same model share the same blind spots and can agree on the same mistake. Use different models to make their mistakes less alike.
- The Tally is an agent too. It judges what counts as the same answer, and it can be wrong.
- If half of the voters or fewer agree, Agent Atlas says there is no majority. The result is still the Tally's choice.

**How it differs from the others**

- **Parallel:** every agent does a different part of the job. In voting every agent does the whole job.
- **Debate:** the agents read each other and argue. Voters never see each other.
- **Evaluator loop:** one agent checks another and asks for a rewrite. Voting only compares and does not improve the answer.

The decision is a JSON answer that the program reads. If it is not valid, the Tally is asked once more, and a second bad answer fails the run.

{related: create-pattern, create-router, create-limits}

### Map-reduce {#create-mapreduce}

A Splitter cuts the task into two to six independent parts. One Worker does each part, all at the same time, and a Reducer merges the results into one answer. In the diagram the Worker is one box with a badge: at run time it runs once for every part, as Worker 1, Worker 2 and so on.

**Why it helps**

- Big jobs that fall apart into independent pieces get done at the same time, so the run is not slower for every extra part.
- Each Worker sees only its own part, so a long task does not crowd one agent's context.

**What it costs and where it fails**

- **Cost:** every part is a full answer, plus the Splitter and the Reducer. Six parts cost eight answers.
- **The cut can be bad.** If the parts depend on each other, the Workers cannot use each other's results and the Reducer has to patch the gaps.
- The Splitter is an agent too. Its reply is a JSON list of two to six parts. If it is not valid, the Splitter is asked once more, and a second bad answer fails the run.

**How it differs from the others**

- **Parallel:** the agents are fixed in the design. In map-reduce the number of Workers is decided by the Splitter during the run.
- **Voting:** every voter does the whole job. A Worker does one part only.
- **Plan-and-execute:** the steps run one after another and see each other. Workers run side by side and never see each other.

{related: create-pattern, create-planexec, create-limits}

### Plan-and-execute {#create-planexec}

A Planner writes a plan of two to six steps once. An Executor then carries out the steps one after another. Each step sees the plan and the results of the earlier steps. The last step is the answer. In the diagram the Executor is one box with a badge, and a dashed arrow marks that it repeats.

**Why it helps**

- The plan stays on screen and shows how far the run is.
- The Executor does one small thing at a time and does not have to hold the whole job in mind.

**What it costs and where it fails**

- **Cost:** one answer for the plan and one for every step. Six steps cost seven answers, and every step reads the earlier results.
- **The plan is never changed during the run.** If step 2 shows that the plan was wrong, the later steps still follow it.
- If a limit stops the run, the result is the text of the last finished step.
- The Planner's reply is a JSON list of two to six steps. If it is not valid, the Planner is asked once more, and a second bad answer fails the run.

**How it differs from the others**

- **Map-reduce:** the parts are independent and run at the same time. The steps here depend on each other and run in order.
- **Handoff:** the agents decide during the run who goes next. Here the plan is fixed before the first step.
- **Evaluator loop:** one agent checks another and asks for a rewrite. Plan-and-execute does not check, it only carries out.

{related: create-pattern, create-mapreduce, create-limits}

### Self-critique {#create-selfcritique}

In the evaluator loop the switch "The Writer judges its own draft" removes the evaluator. The Writer writes a draft, then answers a second time as its own judge, and rewrites if it is not satisfied. In the diagram there is one box with a dashed arrow back to itself.

**Why it helps**

- It is cheaper: there is one agent and one model, and no second agent to set up.

**What it costs and where it fails**

- **A model tends to go easy on its own text.** It shares the blind spots that made the mistake, so it often accepts a draft that another agent would reject. Use a separate evaluator, ideally of another model, when the answer matters.
- The rounds and the criteria work as in the normal loop. The Writer judges against the same criteria.
- Switching it on removes the evaluator and its arrow. Switching it off puts them back.

**How it differs from the others**

- **Evaluator loop:** a different agent judges. With the switch on, the same agent judges its own draft.

{related: create-pattern, create-limits}

### Providers {#create-provider}

A provider is the company whose models the agents use: Claude (Anthropic), Gemini on the free tier or Gemini on a paid key. Switch on one or more. Nothing is chosen for you. A provider without a key is greyed out and takes you to Settings to add one.

Then choose the default model. New agents start with it, and in the table in step 3 you can give any agent a model of another provider you switched on. Switching a provider off removes its models, so you choose the default model again.

{related: create-pattern, create-memory}

### Memory {#create-memory}

By default an agent knows only what is sent to it: the task and the answers of the agents before it. Memory management lets you change that for one run. Everything is off until you switch it on, so a run without it works exactly as before.

**The shared notebook**

The notebook is a list of short notes that exists only while the run lasts. It is like a sheet of paper that lies between the agents: one agent writes a note on it, and an agent that works later can read it. When the run ends, the notebook is gone. Nothing is carried to the next run.

How it works, step by step:

1. You switch on **Shared notebook** for the run, and for each agent you choose what it may do with it (the four choices below).
2. An agent that may **write** is told to put what the others must know at the end of its answer, wrapped in a pair of note tags (an opening and a closing tag with the word note in it), so the program can find them.
3. When the agent has answered, the program takes the notes out of the answer and adds them to the notebook. The notes do not stay in the text that goes to the next agent or into the final result. The replay still shows the whole answer exactly as the model wrote it.
4. An agent that may **read** gets the notes written so far at the end of its input, under the heading *Notebook*. It sees only the notes that exist at the moment it starts.
5. In the replay the notebook is a panel that grows as the notes are written, with the author of each note.

**What each choice means**

- **None.** The agent does not use the notebook. It does not see the notes and cannot write any. It works as it would with the notebook switched off.
- **Read.** The agent sees the notes written before it started, but it cannot add any. Good for an agent that only uses what others found, for example the Writer.
- **Write.** The agent can leave notes, but it does not see the notebook. Good for an agent that should work on its own and only report what it found, for example a Researcher.
- **Read and write.** The agent sees the notes and adds its own. This is the choice new agents start with. Good for an agent in the middle, for example a Critic.

The choice only decides what an agent may do. It does not change who works when: that is still decided by the pattern. An agent whose reply the program reads as a decision (a Supervisor, an Evaluator, a Router, a Tally, or any agent in a handoff) can only read, because a note in its reply could break the decision.

**When it helps**

- A later agent would otherwise do again what an earlier one has already done, for example look up the same fact.
- Agents that work at the same time should not do the same thing twice. A note helps only those that start after it was written.
- An important detail would be lost in a long answer. A short note keeps it.

**Limits of the notebook**

- A note is cut at 600 characters. An agent reads at most the last 6000 characters of the notebook; older notes drop out of what it reads (a warning says so) but stay in the replay.
- The model decides what to write. It can write a poor note or forget to write one.
- Notes add input tokens to every agent that reads them. The Context tab in the replay shows how many.

**Condensing**

An agent can get a short summary of what earlier agents wrote instead of the whole text. You switch it on for one agent with **Condense what this agent receives**. Before the agent starts, the program makes one extra model call with the fast model of the same provider. It keeps the facts, names, numbers and decisions and writes at most 150 words. The agent reads that summary instead of the long text.

- The task itself is never shortened. Only what other agents passed on is, and only when it is longer than 1500 characters. A shorter text is passed on as it is, and nothing is called.
- The extra call counts as a step and its tokens count against the limits and the cost cap, like any other call. The estimate adds one step for each agent that condenses.
- Anything the summary leaves out is lost to that agent. The replay shows what was shortened, what the agent got instead, and the full text on demand.
- An agent whose reply the program reads as a decision (a Supervisor, an Evaluator, a Router or a Tally) cannot condense, because it must see everything it judges. Nor can any agent in a handoff or an evaluator loop, where the text must stay exactly as it is.
- A one-line instruction at the end of an input (for example Give your argument now) is kept as it is, and an instruction from a supervisor is never shortened.
- If the extra call fails, or the agent has fewer than two steps or too few tokens left of its own limits, the agent reads the full text and the replay shows a warning.
- Condensing saves tokens only when the text is long. For short answers it costs more than it saves.

**What it does not do**

- Nothing is remembered between runs.
- The agents do not decide on their own what to remember: the program reads and writes the notebook.

{related: create-pattern, create-limits, replay-context}

### Creating agents {#create-creating}

A Supervisor or a Planner can be allowed to create new agents while the run is going. You set the cap, from 1 to 4. The agent decides what each new agent is and does, and then uses it like any other agent. New agents are Free agents: they can not create agents themselves, and they can not search the web unless you allow it here.

Real systems usually leave the roles to the agent. The guidance field is a teaching aid: it makes runs more repeatable and keeps the cost down, but it also takes away some of the freedom you may want to see. Leave it empty to watch the agent decide alone.

The estimate counts every agent you allow as used, so it is an upper bound. If the agent asks for more than the cap, for a name that is taken, or gives no instructions, the request is refused and the agent is told why.

{related: create-approval}

### Answer format {#answer-format}

An agent can be asked to answer in a fixed format: one JSON object with 1 to 5 named fields. Each field is a text, a number, yes or no, or a list of texts. You set it in the tab Format of the agent. It is off by default.

After every answer Agent Atlas checks the format. A broken answer gets one repair message that names the problems, and the agent answers again. If the second answer is also wrong, the agent fails with the error format.invalid, and Retry works as for any failed agent. A correct answer is passed on to the next agent as compact JSON, with only the fields you named.

The replay shows each check as a step of the agent: a tick when the answer fits, a cross with the problems when it does not. Statistics count the repairs. The estimate counts one extra call for each agent with a format, the worst case.

Agents whose reply the program already reads as a decision (Supervisor, Evaluator, Router, Tally, Splitter, the Planner of plan-and-execute and the agents of a handoff) have no Format tab.

{related: harness-format, create-creating}

### Guardrails {#guardrails}

A guardrail is a check around one agent. It looks at what the agent receives (the task and the note of the agents before it) or at what it answers. You add up to three in the tab Guardrails of the agent. They are off by default.

There are four kinds. **Looks like a secret** finds known shapes only: API keys of the main providers, private key headers, card numbers, and text such as password: followed by a value. **Forbidden words** matches whole words and phrases, capitals ignored. **Too long** fails above a number of characters you choose. **Reviewing model** asks a second model to judge the text against a rule you write. It sees only the text and the rule, never the task, and answers pass or block with a reason. It is one more call, so it costs steps, tokens and money, and it can be fooled like any model. If it gives no clear verdict, the text is blocked.

You choose what happens when a guardrail fails. On an answer: ask the agent to try again once (the message names the reason), stop the run, or only make a note. On what the agent receives: stop (before any call, so nothing is spent) or only make a note. The format check of an answer runs first; the guardrails check the answer after it fits. If an agent that was asked to try again fails again, it stops with the error guardrail.blocked, and Retry asks the model again.

The replay shows each guardrail as a step with the reason and the text that was checked. Statistics count the guardrails that failed, and the estimate counts the extra calls, the worst case. A guardrail on what an agent receives does not see the results of a web search: they stay inside the provider's call. Put a guardrail on the answer of an agent that searches, and read the Learn article Prompt injection.

Agents whose reply the program already reads as a decision (Supervisor, Evaluator, Router, Tally, Splitter, the Planner of plan-and-execute and the agents of a handoff) have no Guardrails tab.

{related: answer-format, create-approval, harness-format}

### Approval {#create-approval}

Switch on **Ask me before this agent runs** for an agent you want to check first, for example before a step that costs a lot or writes the final answer. The run stops before that agent and waits for your click. While it waits nothing runs and nothing is paid. The agents that already finished keep their answers.

If an agent is switched on for approval, each run asks once for it. A run that is waiting is saved, so you can come back to it from Play.

{related: play-approval, create-limits}

### Handoff {#create-handoff}

In a handoff there is no boss. One agent, the one marked **Start**, works on the task. Then it either hands control to another agent it has an arrow to, or finishes the whole task. An arrow means **may hand over to**. Control only moves forward: nothing stands above the agents, and it comes back only if an agent hands it back. This is different from a supervisor, where every result returns to the boss, and from parallel work, where several agents work at once. Here only one agent works at a time.

**What the next agent gets.** Agent Atlas gives the next agent two things: the original task and the note of the agent before it. Nothing else. So the note is all the next agent knows about the work so far. A poor note leaves it almost blind, and the replay shows exactly what each agent received.

**All the ways of passing information at a handoff**

- **The whole history.** The next agent knows everything. Simple, but the cost grows with every handoff, the note stops mattering, and private details leak from agent to agent.
- **The note only.** The strictest. The next agent knows only the note, not even the task. It shows how much depends on the note, but an agent cannot judge the work without the assignment.
- **The note plus the original task (Agent Atlas).** The note is the only record of what happened, so a poor note shows at once. The task keeps the agent from being completely blind. This is why Agent Atlas uses it.
- **A summary of the history.** A model or a rule shortens what came before. Cheaper than the whole history, but the summary can lose what matters. Not available in Agent Atlas (see the Learn article on memory: summaries).
- **Shared notes.** Agents read and write a common set of notes. Flexible, but facts leak between agents and the notes can be tampered with. Not available in Agent Atlas (see the Learn article on memory: shared scratchpad).

**Good to know**

- An agent that gets control again remembers its own earlier turns, but never the turns of other agents.
- The setting **Size of a handoff** (Settings, Harness) cuts a note that is too long and says so in the replay.
- The decision is a JSON answer that the program reads. It is not a tool call of the model.
- **Ping-pong:** A hands to B, B hands back to A, and nobody decides that the work is done. Only the limits (steps, repeated answers, cost) stop it, and a run stopped by a limit has not finished: its result is the last note, not a final answer.

{related: harness-handoff, create-pattern, create-limits}

## When a run fails

- The agent that failed blinks red in the diagram and has a red **Retry** button, in Create right after the run and when you open the failed run from Play or Result.
- **Retry** carries the run on from that agent: the agents that finished keep their answers and are not paid for again, and the new events are added to the same saved run. Its message tells what this try took and cost, then the cost of the whole run.
- The arrow next to Retry opens a list of models, Anthropic and Gemini, with the one that failed marked, and the one the harness suggests marked suggested (a model of the same provider; see Fallback model in Settings, Harness, Fallback model). Pick one to retry that agent with another model, for example when a model is busy. If you set Fallback model to Switch automatically, an agent that is out of tries gets one more try with that model by itself, and the replay says so.
- Under the model list, **Use current limits from Settings** is checked by default: the retry runs with the limits you have in Settings now. Uncheck it to keep the limits the run had. **limit details** shows both sets side by side, and when the Settings limits would probably stop the run before it finishes, a button opens the Harness lab with this run and those limits. The choice is for this Retry only.
- If the key is not in this tab, Create opens with the key fields and a **Carry on the failed run** button. Important messages also show in a message in the middle of the screen that stays until you close it.
- Warnings in a replay show the date and time they happened. A request that gets no answer in time ends with a timeout message that names the provider; you can change how long to wait in Settings.

## Settings

Settings has tabs on the left:

- **API keys**: one tab for each provider. Gemini has a free key and a paid key, so both can be saved at once (a Free key / Paid key switch). Each provider has a color; model tags in replays use it, a cheaper model a paler shade and a stronger one a darker shade. Your keys stay in this browser tab by default. You can save them on this computer, encrypted with a passphrase; one passphrase unlocks every saved key.
- **Harness**: the settings of a run, on the pages Limits, Retries, Fallback model, Cost, Guards and Tools (each setting has a ? button that explains it). Fixed harness lists the rules that always apply. See the next topic.
- **Player controls**: the player buttons beside the diagram or in a slim bar under it.
- **General**: turn off the tooltips (the message in the middle of the screen when a run ends) and the ? help buttons.
- **Data**: back up and restore your runs and settings, or delete them.
- **Shortcuts**: turn keyboard shortcuts on or off and change the go-to letters.
- **Security**: unlock, change or forget the passphrase of a saved key. A red exclamation mark shows when a saved key needs unlocking.

**An arrow is a rule.** An agent may hand over only to an agent it has an arrow to. If it tries to hand over to any other agent, the handoff is refused: the replay shows the refusal as a red step of that agent, the agent is told why and may try once more, and if it asks again the run ends with an error that names both agents. A refused handoff costs a model call but changes nothing in the run. This is the rule of least privilege: draw only the arrows that are really needed.

### API keys {#settings-keys}

Your API keys let the site call the models for you. Each provider has its own tab, and Gemini has a free and a paid key, so both can be saved. By default a key stays in this browser tab and is gone when you close it. You can save it on this computer, encrypted with a passphrase; one passphrase unlocks every saved key.

The button Check models asks a provider which models it offers to a key, and Check all models does it for every key that is loaded. The result is only shown: it marks a model of the app that the provider no longer offers, and nothing is changed or saved.

Keys are used only for the calls to the providers. The site never stores them anywhere else.

{related: settings-security, harness-cost}

### Groq and free accounts {#groq}

Groq runs open models very fast, and its free plan costs nothing, but it has limits: a number of requests and of tokens a minute and a day, counted for each model and for your whole account, not for each key. In Settings, Keys, the Groq tab lists them before you save a key. On Groq, Strong and Balanced are the same model, and web search is not available in this version.

In Create, when an agent runs on a free Groq key, the cost panel compares the run with those limits. It says that the run fits, that it may be slow (the app will wait between calls), that it may not finish today, or that one agent can never run because a single call is bigger than the minute limit. The numbers are an upper bound, so they say "may". Each warning comes with offers: Groq only for the lightest agents, shorter answers and handoffs, letting the run go slowly, running now and continuing tomorrow, moving an agent to another provider you have a key for, fewer voters or rounds, or a paid key.

While a run goes, Groq may answer "429, too many requests". If it asks for a short wait, the app waits (up to a minute, a few times) and says so in a warning. If it asks for a long wait, the day limit is used up and the run stops with everything done so far saved. Continue it after the limit resets. The API calls tab of the replay shows Groq's exact answer.

{related: settings-keys, create-provider, create-estimate}

### OpenRouter, free and paid {#openrouter}

OpenRouter is one address for many models. AAA offers it as two key slots with the same three choices, Fast, Balanced and Strong: OpenRouter free, whose models cost nothing, and OpenRouter paid, which bills your credits. Each slot has its own key, so you can keep both.

The free slot has limits for your whole account: 20 requests a minute and 50 a day (1000 a day once you have bought 10 credits), and no limit on tokens. Its models are served by other companies and may keep and train on what you send, so do not send private data. Web search is not available on OpenRouter in this version.

Free models come and go. Before a run on the free slot, AAA looks at OpenRouter's public list, when you switch the slot on in Create and when Create opens with it on, and the Check models button in Settings does the same. If a model of the registry is gone, AAA proposes nothing itself: it lists the free models that fit, and you pick one for that choice. Your pick is kept in Settings, and recorded runs keep the model they ran on. If the list cannot be reached, you see a warning and the run is allowed.

When OpenRouter answers 429, the app waits if the wait is short. If the day limit is used up, the run stops with everything done so far saved. Continue it after the limit resets. The API calls tab of the replay shows the exact answer.

{related: settings-keys, create-provider, groq, create-estimate}

### Cloudflare Workers AI {#cloudflare}

Cloudflare Workers AI runs open models, and it is offered here as two key slots with the same three choices, Fast, Balanced and Strong: Cloudflare free and Cloudflare paid. The key is one field with two parts, your account ID, a colon and an API token: ACCOUNT_ID:TOKEN. Both are in the Cloudflare dashboard; create the API token with the Workers AI permission.

The free part is not a number of requests but a pool of 10,000 neurons a day, shared by all models and your whole account, and it resets at 00:00 UTC. A neuron is Cloudflare's unit of work: a bigger model, a longer prompt or a longer answer uses more of it. In Settings, Keys, Cloudflare, the table of models shows how many neurons a typical run uses on each model and how many such runs fit in a day. Web search is not available on Cloudflare in this version.

In Create, when an agent runs on a free Cloudflare key, the cost panel compares the run with the pool: it says about how many neurons the run may use, or that it may not finish today, and offers fewer voters or rounds, shorter answers, the lightest model for every agent, running now and continuing tomorrow, moving an agent to another provider you have a key for, or a paid key. The numbers are an upper bound, so they say "may".

When the pool is used up, Cloudflare stops answering, and the run stops with everything done so far saved. Continue it after 00:00 UTC. The API calls tab of the replay shows Cloudflare's exact answer. The Cloudflare pages this app was built from do not say whether prompts are used for training, so do not send private data.

{related: settings-keys, create-provider, create-estimate}

